#N canvas 100 100 640 600 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 100 create;
#X msg 130 100 destroy;
#X msg 250 100 start;
#X msg 340 100 stop;
#X obj 40 150 jet_head 0;
#X obj 40 180 jet_video;
#X obj 40 210 jet_shader slitscan_line.metal;
#X obj 40 240 jet_rectangle 4.2 3.2;
#X msg 250 150 maxdelay 10;
#X msg 340 150 maxdelay 60;
#X msg 250 190 reverse 1;
#X msg 340 190 reverse 0;
#X msg 250 230 freeze 1;
#X msg 340 230 freeze 0;
#X text 40 350 jet_shader slitscan_line.metal - the row-based slit-scan as a shader file. The picture is cut into horizontal bands and each band comes from a different moment: the delay grows from the top to the bottom ("reverse 1" turns it around). "@history 60" in the file keeps the last 60 frames of the input in a GPU texture array \, jet_history() reads the frame N steps back. Parameters (inlets 1 to 4 of the object \, or send them by name as here): maxdelay = how far back the last band reaches (up to 60 frames) \, lines = number of bands (lines 0 = no bands \, every pixel row gets its own delay) \, smooth = 1 blends between neighbouring frames instead of showing whole frames (no steps \, try it together with lines 0) \, reverse. freeze 1 stops recording so you can watch the frozen history scroll by. Edit shaders/slitscan_line.metal while it runs and save - the picture follows.;
#X msg 250 270 lines 12;
#X msg 340 270 lines 0;
#X msg 250 310 smooth 1;
#X msg 340 310 smooth 0;
#X msg 450 150 lines 40;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 6 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 7 0;
#X connect 10 0 7 0;
#X connect 11 0 7 0;
#X connect 12 0 7 0;
#X connect 13 0 7 0;
#X connect 14 0 7 0;
#X connect 16 0 7 0;
#X connect 17 0 7 0;
#X connect 18 0 7 0;
#X connect 19 0 7 0;
#X connect 20 0 7 0;
